*.py text eol=lf
*.js text eol=lf
*.html text eol=lf
*.css text eol=lf
*.sh text eol=lf
*.yml text eol=lf
*.yaml text eol=lf
*.json text eol=lf
# The migration ledger hashes each .sql file's **raw bytes** (`apply_migration.py`
# compares `sha256(path.read_bytes())` against the record it wrote when the file was
# applied). Without this line the extension is left to `core.autocrlf`, so a Windows
# checkout holds CRLF while the Linux box holds LF — one unchanged file with two
# digests, and the box's `--status` reads **APPLIED, THEN EDITED** for a migration
# nobody touched. That verdict was measured here on 12 October 2026 for
# `064_seb_door_refusals.sql` (recorded sha `a0b5f274…` = the same file with LF), which
# is the same false positive this repository had already written down once for `037`.
# Pinned like every other extension above, for the same reason: one file, one identity,
# on every platform that applies it.
*.sql text eol=lf
*.md text eol=lf
*.service text eol=lf
*.timer text eol=lf
# The git hooks carry no extension, so `*.sh` never matched them. Left to
# core.autocrlf they are checked out as CRLF on Windows, and a hook whose first
# line ends with a carriage return is not a shebang — it fails with "bad
# interpreter" on the Linux box that runs it.
deploy/git-hooks/* text eol=lf
